<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      Routing, as a value. No Autodesk reference, and that is the load-bearing part rather than a
      preference: this project is where the search runs, and the search runs on a background thread.

      A modal Revit command owns the API thread, which is also Revit's UI thread - so work done on it
      freezes Revit and freezes our own window with it, and a progress bar that cannot repaint is
      worse than none. The compute phase therefore has to happen off the API thread and touch nothing
      of Revit's.

      The sentence that used to stand here - "the pump does not help either, an ExternalEvent raised
      from inside a modal window never fires, because Revit never reaches Idling while the window is
      up" - was never measured, and half of it is wrong. Measured on all four releases: the pump
      indeed does not run while the window is up, but work posted to it is not lost - it runs the
      moment the window closes, in the same hundredth of a second, because Execute drains the whole
      queue. Revit also still answers reads from inside the window, and an await taken there resumes
      on the API thread. None of that changes this project's shape; it changes the command's, which
      applies in the continuation rather than handing the work to the pump and closing the window
      before the work it describes is done.

      "Touch nothing of Revit's" is a rule somebody eventually breaks, so it is not left to
      discipline: on this axis the type Document does not exist. A missing field in the snapshot
      stops being a Revit that hangs in front of a customer and becomes an error at build time, in
      the hour it is written. The same split was chosen to make the routing debuggable without
      Revit; it turns out to be the thread guarantee as well.
    -->

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFrameworks>net48;net8.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
        <RootNamespace>BHS.MEP.Cabling.Routing</RootNamespace>
    </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
